---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About ShouldISpend"
  description="Learn what ShouldISpend is, how its calculators work, and why the site uses a conservative, context-driven approach to spending decisions."
>
  <style>
    .container {
      max-width: 860px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 80px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: 4rem;
      line-height: 1;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
      margin: 56px 0 18px;
      color: var(--navy);
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.08rem;
      line-height: 1.85;
    }

    p {
      margin-bottom: 22px;
    }

    ul {
      padding-left: 24px;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
    }

    a {
      color: var(--teal);
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .note {
      background: #f8fafc;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 20px;
      padding: 26px;
      margin-top: 36px;
    }

    .intro {
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .divider {
      height: 1px;
      background: var(--border);
      margin: 48px 0;
    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
      h1 {
        font-size: 2.9rem;
      }

      h2 {
        font-size: 1.7rem;
      }

      .container {
        padding: 64px 20px;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">About ShouldISpend</div>

    <h1>Spend With Context.</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      ShouldISpend is a decision-focused financial education website designed to
      help people think through major purchases before committing to them.
    </p>

    <p>
      The site was created by Dustin Baker, a longtime editor and writer based in
      Sioux Falls, South Dakota. Instead of treating every spending decision as
      simply “good” or “bad,” ShouldISpend focuses on pressure, flexibility,
      recovery time, and long-term financial comfort.
    </p>

    <p>
      Many budgeting tools only answer whether a person can technically afford
      something. ShouldISpend tries to answer a different question:
      <strong>Will this still feel manageable after the money is gone?</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>Why the Site Exists</h2>

    <p>
      Large purchases are rarely just math problems. Weddings, apartments,
      vacations, car payments, debt decisions, emergency expenses, and lifestyle
      upgrades often involve stress, emotion, uncertainty, and tradeoffs.
    </p>

    <p>
      ShouldISpend was built to slow those decisions down and add context before
      someone commits to a major financial obligation.
    </p>

    <p>
      The site combines calculators, plain-language explanations, financial
      pressure analysis, and educational guides to help users better understand
      how a spending decision may affect their broader financial life.
    </p>

    <p>
      The goal is not perfection. The goal is clarity.
    </p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>How ShouldISpend Works</h2>

    <p>
      Most calculators on the site evaluate a mix of financial factors, including:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Income and monthly cash flow</li>
      <li>Savings and emergency reserves</li>
      <li>Debt obligations and monthly payments</li>
      <li>Total purchase size or recurring payment burden</li>
      <li>Financial recovery time after the purchase</li>
      <li>Risk of relying on high-interest debt</li>
      <li>Remaining flexibility after the expense</li>
      <li>Long-term pressure on day-to-day finances</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Many calculators also include educational sections, FAQs, scenario-based
      guidance, and contextual explanations to help users understand the “why”
      behind a verdict — not just the score itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      ShouldISpend is intentionally designed to prioritize financial stability,
      emergency flexibility, and sustainable spending habits over impulse
      decision-making.
    </p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>Built Around Practical Verdicts</h2>

    <p>
      ShouldISpend is not a stock-picking website, investing platform, lender,
      or debt-relief company. The site focuses on everyday spending pressure:
      rent, weddings, vacations, groceries, transportation, debt levels,
      emergency savings, and other real-world financial decisions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each calculator is written and structured individually, even when topics
      are closely related. Different spending decisions create different forms of
      pressure, risk, and tradeoffs. A honeymoon calculator should not think the
      same way as a rent calculator. A grocery budget decision should not behave
      like a luxury-car payment decision.
    </p>

    <p>
      That context matters. ShouldISpend is built around that philosophy.
    </p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>Conservative by Design</h2>

    <p>
      ShouldISpend generally leans conservative. That does not mean large
      purchases are automatically bad. Many meaningful life experiences and
      important upgrades cost money and can absolutely be worth it.
    </p>

    <p>
      But the calculators are intentionally cautious about:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Draining emergency savings</li>
      <li>High-interest debt reliance</li>
      <li>Oversized recurring monthly payments</li>
      <li>Financial situations with little room for setbacks</li>
      <li>Spending decisions that create long recovery periods</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      In many cases, financial stress does not come from the purchase itself —
      it comes from having no flexibility left afterward.
    </p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>Educational Use Only</h2>

    <p>
      ShouldISpend provides educational information, estimates, and generalized
      decision-support tools. The content on this website is not financial,
      legal, tax, insurance, medical, or investment advice.
    </p>

    <p>
      The calculators use generalized assumptions and cannot account for every
      personal circumstance, regional cost difference, family obligation, tax
      situation, career risk, or emergency scenario.
    </p>

    <p>
      Users should always apply personal judgment and, when appropriate, consult
      qualified professionals before making major financial decisions.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        ShouldISpend is designed to be a practical educational resource — not a
        substitute for licensed financial planning or individualized professional
        advice.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>About the Creator</h2>

    <p>
      Dustin Baker is a longtime writer and editor who has spent years creating
      large-scale informational and editorial content online. ShouldISpend was
      built as a long-term project focused on helping people think more clearly
      about real-world spending decisions.
    </p>

    <p>
      The site continues to expand with new calculators, financial explainers,
      budgeting tools, and educational resources designed around practical
      decision-making instead of financial hype.
    </p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <h2>Learn More</h2>

    <p>
      Visit the <a href="/calculators">Calculators</a> page to explore tools
      across weddings, groceries, rent, travel, debt, and lifestyle spending.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can also review the <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a> page or
      reach out through the <a href="/contact">Contact</a> page.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>